WEBVTT

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.200
We live in the data revolution.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.800
The agri-food sector constantly generates information through sensors, soil analyses, climate data and satellites.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:19.680
However, this data is heterogeneous, multisensory and scattered, and an isolated piece of data has very limited value.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:29.880
The real impact comes when we share it and, building on it, jointly develop services that bring real value to decision-making.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:35.290
Soil Data Space is a federated soil data space.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:46.930
It is an interoperable and secure infrastructure in which each organisation keeps sovereignty over its information and decides how to share it under common governance rules.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:56.890
The project is driven by a multidisciplinary team of computer scientists, biologists, geomatics experts and soil science specialists.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:01.470
This combination makes it possible to tackle the challenge from the technological side

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:04.710
as well as from scientific and territorial knowledge.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:11.450
The project is built on three core pillars aligned with the European Data Strategy

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.330
and with the model promoted in Spain by the Data Spaces Reference Centre.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.430
First, data sovereignty.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:26.210
Each organisation keeps control of its information and decides how, and under what conditions, it is shared.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:31.210
Second, interoperability, based on European standards

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:36.490
and on a federated architecture that ensures security, traceability and trust.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:44.490
And finally, the deployment of services, which turns information into useful applications for decision-making

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.490
and creates real value for the agri-food sector.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:54.490
These strategic pillars become meaningful when they are turned into real solutions.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:59.490
The value of the project lies in its ability to turn data into applications and services

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:00.990
that meet the needs of society

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.490
and the specific needs of the agri-environmental sector,

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:09.490
improving management, optimising resources and creating new opportunities.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:16.490
Greenhouse gas emissions keep rising, and soil can be part of the solution.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:23.490
The CLIMA use case provides tools to monitor and quantify soil organic carbon,

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:29.490
making its certification easier and actively helping to mitigate climate change.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:35.490
Soil erosion threatens the sustainability of the land and agricultural productivity.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:41.490
The HIDRO use case makes it possible to assess and map soil loss accurately,

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:46.490
supporting land-use planning and the design of effective mitigation measures.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:51.490
Agriculture must move towards more efficient and resilient models.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:57.490
The AGRI use case drives data-based decision-making to optimise crop management

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:58.990
and improve sustainable soil management.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:04.490
A new era in soil management has already begun.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:10.490
Join our data space and turn your data into high-value services.
